Let $f_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandU\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringU\endcsname}\else\bm{U}\fi
}(\bm{s})$ be a function of codewords $\bm{s}$, induced by the modulation basis $ 
  \ifcat\noexpandU\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringU\endcsname}\else\bm{U}\fi
$. We say that $f_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandU\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringU\endcsname}\else\bm{U}\fi
}(\bm{s})$ is type-based controllable, if there exists a function $h_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandU\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringU\endcsname}\else\bm{U}\fi
}:{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandP\relax\mathnormal{P}\else\cal{P}\fi
}_N({ 
  \ifcat\noexpandS\relax\mathnormal{S}\else\cal{S}\fi
})\rightarrow \mathbb{R}$ of types, such that
\begin{equation}\label{controllability}
f_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandU\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringU\endcsname}\else\bm{U}\fi
}(\bm{s})=h_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandU\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringU\endcsname}\else\bm{U}\fi
}(\hat{P}_{\bm{s}}),\qquad\bm{s}\neq \bm{0}.
\end{equation}

\label{prop:perm}
For $\bm{s}\in\mathbb{C}^N\backslash\{\bm{0}\}$, the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item There exists $h_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandU\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringU\endcsname}\else\bm{U}\fi
}$ such that \eqref{controllability} holds;
\item For any permutation matrix $ 
  \ifcat\noexpand\Pi\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\string\Pi\endcsname}\else\bm{\Pi}\fi
$ and non-zero $\bm{s}$, it holds that
\begin{equation}
f_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandU\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringU\endcsname}\else\bm{U}\fi
}( 
  \ifcat\noexpand\Pi\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\string\Pi\endcsname}\else\bm{\Pi}\fi
\bm{s})=f_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandU\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringU\endcsname}\else\bm{U}\fi
}(\bm{s}).
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}
\begin{IEEEproof}
Please refer to Appendix \ref{ssec:proof_perm}.
\end{IEEEproof}

An important special case of modulation bases satisfying \eqref{islr_controllable_u} is \ac{ofdm}, corresponding to $ 
  \ifcat\noexpandU\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringU\endcsname}\else\bm{U}\fi
= 
  \ifcat\noexpandF\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringF\endcsname}\else\bm{F}\fi
_N^{\rm H} 
  \ifcat\noexpandM\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringM\endcsname}\else\bm{M}\fi
$ and $ 
  \ifcat\noexpandC\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringC\endcsname}\else\bm{C}\fi
_{\alpha,\alpha}=e^{j\alpha} 
  \ifcat\noexpandI\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringI\endcsname}\else\bm{I}\fi
$. In the general \ac{islr}-controllable case, we have
\begin{equation}
{\rm ISLR}=\frac{\sum_{s\in{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandS\relax\mathnormal{S}\else\cal{S}\fi
}}q(s)|s+(e^{j(\alpha-\beta)}-1)m_q|^4}{\big(\sum_{s\in{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandS\relax\mathnormal{S}\else\cal{S}\fi
}}q(s)|s|^2\big)^2}-1,
\end{equation}
where $m_q=\sum_{s\in{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandS\relax\mathnormal{S}\else\cal{S}\fi
}}q(s)s$. In light of this, if all types $\hat{q}$ used in the codebook satisfy the exact zero-mean condition $m_{\hat{q}}=0$, all \ac{islr}-controllable modulation bases would yield the same \ac{islr} values as \ac{ofdm} does.

\label{lem:monomial_zeros}
$A_{\rm a}(\bm{s})=0$ if and only if $\bm{s}$ has a single non-zero entry.
\begin{IEEEproof}
Please refer to Appendix \ref{ssec:proof_monomial_zeros}.
\end{IEEEproof}

\label{lem:reversal}
The permutation $\sigma(k)$ is either an identity transformation or a reversal permutation.
\begin{IEEEproof}
Please refer to Appendix \ref{ssec:proof_reversal}.
\end{IEEEproof}

\label{lem:ramp}
The coefficient $\lambda_k$ in \eqref{monomial_result} satisfies
\begin{equation}
\lambda_k = \lambda_0 q^k~{\rm or}~\lambda_{N-1}q^{N-k-1},
\end{equation}
where $|\lambda_0|=|q|=1$.
\begin{IEEEproof}
Please refer to Appendix \ref{ssec:proof_ramp}.
\end{IEEEproof}

\label{thm:nogo_aislr}
When $N\geqslant 4$, there does not exist any unitary $ 
  \ifcat\noexpandU\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringU\endcsname}\else\bm{U}\fi
$ such that \ac{aacf} \ac{islr} is type-based controllable.
\begin{IEEEproof}
Please refer to Appendix \ref{ssec:proof_nogo_aislr}.
\end{IEEEproof}

First note that types themselves are permutation invariant, thereby \eqref{controllability} would imply 2). Conversely, if 2) holds, for any realizable type $q$, we may choose arbitrarily a representative codeword $\bm{s}$ satisfying $\hat{P}_{\bm{s}}=q$, and define $h_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandU\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringU\endcsname}\else\bm{U}\fi
}(q)=J_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandU\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringU\endcsname}\else\bm{U}\fi
}(\bm{s})$. If we choose another representative codeword $\bm{t}$ in the type class ${ 
  \ifcat\noexpandT\relax\mathnormal{T}\else\cal{T}\fi
}_N(q)$, there exists a permutation matrix $ 
  \ifcat\noexpand\Pi\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\string\Pi\endcsname}\else\bm{\Pi}\fi
$ such that $\bm{t}= 
  \ifcat\noexpand\Pi\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\string\Pi\endcsname}\else\bm{\Pi}\fi
\bm{s}$ since all sequences in the same type class differ only by their order of entries. Thus 2) implies $h_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandU\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringU\endcsname}\else\bm{U}\fi
}(\bm{t})=h_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandU\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringU\endcsname}\else\bm{U}\fi
}(\bm{s})$ for all $\bm{t}$ and $\bm{s}$ in the same type class, which in turn implies 1).

Let $\bm{u}_n$ denote the $n$-th column of $ 
  \ifcat\noexpandU\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringU\endcsname}\else\bm{U}\fi
$. The unitarity and $\ell_4$ isometry imply
\begin{equation}\label{unitary_isometry}
\|\bm{u}_n\|_2^2=1,~\|\bm{u}\|_4^4=1.
\end{equation}
In addition, we have
\begin{equation}\label{fourth_expansion}
\|\bm{u}_n\|_2^4=\|\bm{u}_n\|_4^4+2\sum_{i<j}|u_{in}|^2|u_{jn}|^2.
\end{equation}
Combining \eqref{unitary_isometry} and \eqref{fourth_expansion} yields $\sum_{i<j}|u_{in}|^2|u_{jn}|^2=0$, which implies that each column has at most one non-zero entry. Using again the unitarity, we see that $ 
  \ifcat\noexpandU\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringU\endcsname}\else\bm{U}\fi
$ has to be monomial, and we are done.

We examine the two conditions regarding the orbitals of lines separately.
\subsubsection{Condition (a)}
Without loss of generality, assume that ${ 
  \ifcat\noexpandL\relax\mathnormal{L}\else\cal{L}\fi
}_0,\dotsc,{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandL\relax\mathnormal{L}\else\cal{L}\fi
}_{N-1}$ constitute an orbital. On each line ${ 
  \ifcat\noexpandL\relax\mathnormal{L}\else\cal{L}\fi
}_k$, we choose a unit vector $\bm{v}_k$. Since the lines are on the same orbital, the symmetric group acts transitively on these lines, namely,
\begin{equation}
\exists  
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
\in\mathscr{S}_N,~ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandL\relax\mathnormal{L}\else\cal{L}\fi
}_k={ 
  \ifcat\noexpandL\relax\mathnormal{L}\else\cal{L}\fi
}_j,
\end{equation}
for any $j$, $k$. Therefore, for any $ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
\in\mathscr{S}_N$, there exists a complex scalar $|\lambda_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
,k}|=1$ such that $ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
\bm{v}_k=\lambda_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
,k}\bm{v}_{\sigma_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
}(k)}$. On the other hand, being a permutation matrix, any $ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
\in\mathscr{S}_N$ satisfies $ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
\bm{1}=\bm{1}$. The unit vector $\bm{u}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\bm{1}$ thus satisfies
\begin{equation}
\bm{u}^{\rm H}\bm{v}_k=( 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
\bm{u})^{\rm H}\bm{v}_k = \lambda_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
_k}\bm{u}^{\rm H}\bm{v}_{\sigma_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
}(k)}.
\end{equation}
Taking the magnitude from both sides yields
\begin{equation}
|\bm{u}^{\rm H}\bm{v}_k| = |\bm{u}^{\rm H}\bm{v}_{\sigma_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
}(k)}|,
\end{equation}
thereby $\bm{u}$ has constant-modulus projections on all the unit vectors $\{\bm{v}_k\}$. By adjusting the phases of $\bm{v}_k$'s we obtain
\begin{equation}
\bm{u}=\frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}\bm{v}_k.
\end{equation}
Since $ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
\bm{u}=\bm{u}$, it further holds that $\lambda_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
,k}=1$. Therefore the actions of $\mathscr{S}_N$ elements on the lines constitute a natural permutation representation $\tau:\mathscr{S}_N\rightarrow \mathscr{S}_N$, which is an automorphism. For $N\neq 6$, it is known that all automorphisms of $\mathscr{S}_N$ are inner automorphisms \cite[Sec.~8.2]{permgroup}, which implies that there exists a permutation matrix $ 
  \ifcat\noexpand\Xi\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\string\Xi\endcsname}\else\bm{\Xi}\fi
$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{inner_auto}
\tau( 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
)= 
  \ifcat\noexpand\Xi\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\string\Xi\endcsname}\else\bm{\Xi}\fi

  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi

  \ifcat\noexpand\Xi\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\string\Xi\endcsname}\else\bm{\Xi}\fi
^{\rm T},\qquad \forall 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
\in\mathscr{S}_N.
\end{equation}
Now, let $ 
  \ifcat\noexpandB\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringB\endcsname}\else\bm{B}\fi
=[\bm{v}_0,\dotsc,\bm{v}_{N-1}]$, we see that there exists a unitary diagonal matrix $ 
  \ifcat\noexpandD\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringD\endcsname}\else\bm{D}\fi
$ such that $ 
  \ifcat\noexpandB\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringB\endcsname}\else\bm{B}\fi
= 
  \ifcat\noexpandV\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringV\endcsname}\else\bm{V}\fi
^{\rm H} 
  \ifcat\noexpandD\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringD\endcsname}\else\bm{D}\fi
$, since $\bm{v}_k\in 
  \ifcat\noexpandV\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringV\endcsname}\else\bm{V}\fi
^{\rm H}{\rm span}(\bm{e}_k)$. Choosing an appropriate $\sigma$ such that $\tau( 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
)\bm{e}_k=\bm{e}_{\sigma_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
}(k)}$, we have
\begin{align}

  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi

  \ifcat\noexpandB\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringB\endcsname}\else\bm{B}\fi
\bm{e}_k&= 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi

  \ifcat\noexpandV\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringV\endcsname}\else\bm{V}\fi
^{\rm H} 
  \ifcat\noexpandD\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringD\endcsname}\else\bm{D}\fi
\bm{e}_k\nonumber\\
&=[\bm{v}_{\sigma_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
}(0)},~\dotsc,~\bm{v}_{\sigma_{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
}(N-1)}] 
  \ifcat\noexpandD\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringD\endcsname}\else\bm{D}\fi
\bm{e}_k\nonumber \\
&= 
  \ifcat\noexpandV\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringV\endcsname}\else\bm{V}\fi
^{\rm H} 
  \ifcat\noexpandD\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringD\endcsname}\else\bm{D}\fi
\tau( 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
)\bm{e}_k\nonumber \\
&= 
  \ifcat\noexpandB\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringB\endcsname}\else\bm{B}\fi
\tau( 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
)\bm{e}_k,
\end{align}
and hence $ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi

  \ifcat\noexpandB\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringB\endcsname}\else\bm{B}\fi
= 
  \ifcat\noexpandB\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringB\endcsname}\else\bm{B}\fi
\tau( 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
)$. Using \eqref{inner_auto}, we obtain
\begin{equation}

  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi

  \ifcat\noexpandB\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringB\endcsname}\else\bm{B}\fi

  \ifcat\noexpand\Xi\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\string\Xi\endcsname}\else\bm{\Xi}\fi
= 
  \ifcat\noexpandB\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringB\endcsname}\else\bm{B}\fi

  \ifcat\noexpand\Xi\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\string\Xi\endcsname}\else\bm{\Xi}\fi

  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
.
\end{equation}
This implies that $ 
  \ifcat\noexpandB\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringB\endcsname}\else\bm{B}\fi

  \ifcat\noexpand\Xi\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\string\Xi\endcsname}\else\bm{\Xi}\fi
$ is a centralizer of $ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
\in\mathscr{S}_N$. The following lemma gives a characterization of such centralizers.
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:commutation}
If a unitary matrix $ 
  \ifcat\noexpandC\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringC\endcsname}\else\bm{C}\fi
$ commutes with all permutation matrices in $\mathscr{S}_N$, we have
\begin{equation}\label{c_ab}

  \ifcat\noexpandC\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringC\endcsname}\else\bm{C}\fi
= 
  \ifcat\noexpandC\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringC\endcsname}\else\bm{C}\fi
_{\alpha,\beta}:=e^{j\beta} 
  \ifcat\noexpandI\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringI\endcsname}\else\bm{I}\fi
+\frac{1}{N}(e^{j\alpha}-e^{j\beta})\bm{1}\bm{1}^{\rm T}.
\end{equation}
\begin{IEEEproof}
Using the classical orbital basis result on the centralizer algebra of permutation representations \cite[Sec.~2.2]{cameron_permgroup}, we obtain
\begin{equation}

  \ifcat\noexpandC\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringC\endcsname}\else\bm{C}\fi
\in{\rm span}\{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandI\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringI\endcsname}\else\bm{I}\fi
,\bm{1}\bm{1}^{\rm T}\}.
\end{equation}
Using the unitarity of $ 
  \ifcat\noexpandC\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringC\endcsname}\else\bm{C}\fi
$, it follows that the eigenvalues on ${\rm span}(\bm{1})$ and its orthogonal complement are both unimodular, thus leading to \eqref{c_ab}.
\end{IEEEproof}
Using Lemma \ref{lem:commutation}, we finally obtain
\begin{equation}

  \ifcat\noexpandV\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringV\endcsname}\else\bm{V}\fi
= 
  \ifcat\noexpandD\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringD\endcsname}\else\bm{D}\fi

  \ifcat\noexpand\Xi\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\string\Xi\endcsname}\else\bm{\Xi}\fi

  \ifcat\noexpandC\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringC\endcsname}\else\bm{C}\fi
_{\alpha,\beta}^{\rm H}= 
  \ifcat\noexpandD\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringD\endcsname}\else\bm{D}\fi

  \ifcat\noexpand\Xi\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\string\Xi\endcsname}\else\bm{\Xi}\fi

  \ifcat\noexpandC\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringC\endcsname}\else\bm{C}\fi
_{-\alpha,-\beta},
\end{equation}
where $ 
  \ifcat\noexpandD\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringD\endcsname}\else\bm{D}\fi

  \ifcat\noexpand\Xi\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\string\Xi\endcsname}\else\bm{\Xi}\fi
\in\mathsf{Mon}(N)$. 
\end{lemma}

\subsubsection{Condition (b)}
In this case, the fact that the orbital contains $N-1$ lines implies that there is a homomorphism $\varphi:\mathscr{S}_N\rightarrow\mathscr{S}_{N-1}$. When $N\geqslant 5$, $\mathscr{S}_N$ has a non-trivial normal subgroup, the alternating group $\mathscr{A}_N$. Since $\varphi$ is not injective, we have $\mathscr{A}_N\subseteq {\rm Ker}~\varphi$ for $N\geqslant 5$. Thus using the basic theorem of homomorphisms, we have
\begin{equation}
|{\rm Im}~\varphi|\leqslant \frac{|\mathscr{S}_N|}{|\mathscr{A}_N|}=2.
\end{equation}
However, in Condition (b) the orbital size argument yields $|{\rm Im}~\varphi|\geqslant N-1$, which is clearly impossible for $N\geqslant 5$. Therefore, orbitals satisfying Condition (b) do not exist when $N\geqslant 5$.

The full proof is thus completed by combining the analysis of Conditions (a) and (b).

Using the classical orbital basis result on the centralizer algebra of permutation representations \cite[Sec.~2.2]{cameron_permgroup}, we obtain
\begin{equation}

  \ifcat\noexpandC\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringC\endcsname}\else\bm{C}\fi
\in{\rm span}\{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandI\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringI\endcsname}\else\bm{I}\fi
,\bm{1}\bm{1}^{\rm T}\}.
\end{equation}
Using the unitarity of $ 
  \ifcat\noexpandC\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringC\endcsname}\else\bm{C}\fi
$, it follows that the eigenvalues on ${\rm span}(\bm{1})$ and its orthogonal complement are both unimodular, thus leading to \eqref{c_ab}.

Note that
\begin{align}
\|\bm{s}\|_4^4 &= \|\bm{s}\|_2^2+A_{\rm a}(\bm{s})\nonumber \\
&=\sum_{a+b=c+d\atop 0\leqslant a,b,c,d<N}s_as_bs_c^*s_d^*. \label{fourth_monomials}
\end{align}
Preservation of $A_{\rm a}$ is thus equivalent to the preservation of $\|\bm{s}\|_4$ since $\|\bm{s}\|_2^4$ is already preserved due to unitarity. Now, substituting \eqref{monomial_result} into \eqref{fourth_monomials}, we obtain
\begin{equation}
\sigma(a)+\sigma(b)=\sigma(c)+\sigma(d).
\end{equation}
Using $0+n=1+(n-1)$, we further obtain
\begin{equation}
\sigma(n)-\sigma(n-1)=\sigma(1)-\sigma(0):=a,
\end{equation}
which further implies that
\begin{equation}\label{affine_perm}
\sigma(n)=\sigma(0)+a.
\end{equation}
Since $\sigma(k)$ is a bijection, $a$ is a non-zero integer. Thus \eqref{affine_perm} implies $(N-1)|a|\le N-1$, thereby $a=\pm 1$. Thus we have
\begin{equation}
\sigma(n)=n~{\rm or}~\sigma(n)=N-1-n,
\end{equation}
which corresponds to the identity and the reversal permutation, respectively. Thus the proof is completed.

First assume that the permutation component is an identity transformation. Using again the identity $0+n=1+(n-1)$ we have the monomial $s_0s_ns_1^*s_{n-1}^*$. Note that all four permitted permutations $(0,n,1,n-1),~(n,0,1,n-1),~(0,n,n-1,1),~(n,0,n-1,1)$ correspond to the same monomial $s_0s_ns_1^*s_{n-1}^*$, and hence we have $4\lambda_0\lambda_n\lambda_1^*\lambda_{n-1}^*=4$. This implies
\begin{equation}
\frac{\lambda_n}{\lambda_{n-1}} = \frac{\lambda_1}{\lambda_0} := q.
\end{equation}
When the permutation component is the reversal permutation, we may simply alter the indices accordingly. The proof is thus completed.

Let
\begin{equation}
\widetilde{{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\mathnormal{G}\else\cal{G}\fi
}}_N=\{a 
  \ifcat\noexpandD\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringD\endcsname}\else\bm{D}\fi
(q):|a|=|q|=1\},
\end{equation}
which is an Abelian normal subgroup of ${ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\mathnormal{G}\else\cal{G}\fi
}_N$. Note that
\begin{equation}
{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\mathnormal{G}\else\cal{G}\fi
}_N/\widetilde{{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\mathnormal{G}\else\cal{G}\fi
}}_N \cong \mathscr{C}_2,
\end{equation}
where $\mathscr{C}_2$ is the 2-cyclic group accounting for the potential reversal. If \eqref{aacf_islr_control} holds, the commutator subgroup of $\mathscr{S}_N$, the alternating group $\mathscr{A}_N$, would have to be mapped into $\widetilde{{ 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\mathnormal{G}\else\cal{G}\fi
}}_N$ by the conjugate mapping $g: 
  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi
\mapsto  
  \ifcat\noexpandU\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringU\endcsname}\else\bm{U}\fi

  \ifcat\noexpandG\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringG\endcsname}\else\bm{G}\fi

  \ifcat\noexpandU\relax\bm{\csname var\expandafter\@gobble\stringU\endcsname}\else\bm{U}\fi
^{\rm H}$. However, when $N\geqslant 4$, $\mathscr{A}_N$ is non-Abelian and hence cannot be mapped into any Abelian group via the injection $g$. Thus we are done.
